Scenario 10 - Xcode Accessibility Inspector audit (SC3): NOT RUN (INCONCLUSIVE)

Attempted 2026-09-12 ~16:45-16:47 local.

What worked:
- request_access granted for "Accessibility Inspector" (com.apple.AccessibilityInspector)
  and "Simulator" (com.apple.iphonesimulator).
- Accessibility Inspector launched (open_application) and is visible on monitor "Odyssey G65B".
- Its window opened with target = "Halil's MacBook Pro" (the Mac), Inspection tab active.
- Keyboard input reaches the app (a cmd+2 key press was accepted).

What blocked it:
- Every pointer click inside the Inspector - the target picker at (595,185), the
  Inspection/Audit/Settings toggles at top right, and even its own menu bar items
  ("Accessibility Inspector" at (72,8), "Inspection" at (192,8)) - is refused with:
      Click at these coordinates would land on "axAuditService",
      which is not in the allowed applications.
- The Inspector's window contents and menu ownership resolve to its XPC helper
  /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Applications/Accessibility Inspector.app/
    Contents/XPCServices/axAuditService.xpc/Contents/MacOS/axAuditService
  (CFBundleIdentifier com.apple.accessibility.axAuditService, running as pid 43526).
- That helper cannot be added to the computer-use allowlist: request_access for both
  "axAuditService" and "com.apple.accessibility.axAuditService" returns
  notInstalled ("doesn't match any installed or running application") and the request
  is short-circuited without ever being shown to the user.
- Consequence: the audit target cannot be switched from the Mac to the iPhone 17
  simulator, and the Audit tab cannot be reached. No audit was run on any screen,
  so no issue counts or issue text exist to transcribe.

Not attempted, deliberately:
- XCUITest performAccessibilityAudit() - would require authoring a test file, which
  this run is forbidden to do (drive-only mandate).
- AppleScript/System Events UI scripting - would require granting Accessibility
  permission to another process, i.e. changing a system security setting.

Partial substitute evidence actually collected from the live accessibility tree
(agent-device snapshot --raw / --actions). This is NOT the Inspector audit and does
not satisfy SC3; it is recorded only so the gap is precise:
- Recipient and question options expose proper radio semantics:
  value "radio button, checked" / "radio button, unchecked".
- Validation errors are programmatically associated with their control: the step 2
  question label reads "How long have you had this problem? (required). Error: This
  question is required" and the step 3 field reads "What would you like to ask?.
  Error: Please write your question before sending".
- Disabled states are exposed as [disabled] (Continue before a choice; Change,
  Remove photo, Choose from library and Send during a send; Send while offline).
- Every interactive control carried a non-empty label; the photo preview is labelled
  "Your photo"; no unlabelled buttons or images were observed on any screen driven.
